TI  - Modeling Social Networks With Graph Databases for Information Security Analysis
AU  - P. Y. Filyak
AU  - V. Y. Latkin
AU  - L. A. Vitkova
T2  - 2025 International Ural Conference on Electrical Power Engineering (UralCon)
AB  - This paper presents an approach to modeling social networks with graph databases for information security analysis. The study focuses on representing individuals and their interactions as nodes and edges within graph structures, enabling the explicit encoding of both formal and informal relationships. This representation improves the visibility of social dynamics that may influence security, including cooperation, conflict, and malicious intent. The proposed methodology, implemented using the Neo4j graph database and its Cypher query language, encompasses computational schematization, network decomposition, and graph construction with verification of relationships. Beyond individual networks, the approach extends to metagraphs that integrate heterogeneous social, organizational, and socio-technical systems. The results demonstrate that graph-based models can effectively support the analysis of information flows and the detection of fake news, providing a practical foundation for information security management and future large-scale applications.
