TI  - Enhancing Misinformation Detection through Semantic Analysis and Knowledge Graphs
AU  - S. E. V. S. Pillai
T2  - 2024 4th International Conference on Data Engineering and Communication Systems (ICDECS)
AB  - Introducing improvements in technology has caused an exponential increase in the availability and consumption of virtual data. This has also given rise to the spread of misinformation, a problem that has significant consequences for both individuals and society. The ability to correctly locate and combat misinformation is vital in preventing its dangerous outcomes. Traditional methods of detecting misinformation, including truth-checking and source evaluation, have proven to be inadequate in the contemporary digital landscape. To address this problem, researchers have turned to advanced technology, including natural language processing and machine learning, to improve the accuracy and performance of misinformation detection. However, these techniques often depend on surface-level features, such as language patterns and metadata, which can be easily manipulated by those creating and disseminating misinformation. This quandary requires a deeper understanding of the underlying semantic meaning of the facts. Semantic analysis, a branch of natural language processing, focuses on understanding the meaning and context of language. By incorporating this method into misinformation detection, it is possible to go beyond surface-level features and analyze the underlying intent and meaning of the information. This could offer a much better and accurate approach for detecting misinformation. Moreover, the use of knowledge graphs can further enhance this approach by capturing and representing the relationships between entities, ideas, and information.
