TI  - Improved Deepfake Video Detection Using Convolutional Vision Transformer
AU  - D. W. Deressa
AU  - P. Lambert
AU  - G. Van Wallendael
AU  - S. Atnafu
AU  - H. Mareen
T2  - 2024 IEEE Gaming, Entertainment, and Media Conference (GEM)
AB  - Deepfakes are hyper-realistic videos in which the faces are replaced, swapped, or forged using deep-learning models. This potent media manipulation techniques hold promise for applications across various domains. Yet, they also present a significant risk when employed for malicious intents like iden-tity fraud, phishing, spreading false information, and executing scams. In this work, we propose a novel and improved Deepfake video detector that uses a Convolutional Vision Transformer (CViT2), which builds on the concepts of our previous work (CViT). The CViT architecture consists of two components: a Convolutional Neural Network that extracts learnable features, and a Vision Transformer that categorizes these learned features using an attention mechanism. We trained and evaluted our model on 5 datasets, namely Deepfake Detection Challenge Dataset (DFDC), $\mathbf{FaceForensics++} \ (\text{FF}++)$ I, Celeb-DF v2, Deep-fakeTIMIT, and TrustedMedia. On the test sets unseen during training, we achieved an accuracy of 95 %, 94.8 %, 98.3 % and 76.7% on the DFDC, $\mathbf{FF ++}$, Celeb-DF v2, and TIMIT datasets, respectively. In conclusion, our proposed Deepfake detector can be used in the battle against misinformation and other forensic use cases.
